  "text_md": "22 CHRISTIAN KETTERER\n\nsperation function\n\nτ B×f F((p, x), (q, y)) := sup L(γ)\n\nwhere the sup is w.r.t. all future directed causal curves γ that connect (p, x) and (q, y). If there is no such curve, one sets τ ((p, x), (q, y)) = 0. τB×f F satisfies the reverse △-inequality\n\nτ B×f F((p, x), (q, y)) ≥ τB×f F((p, x), (r, z)) + τB×f F((r, z), (q, y)).\n\nFor all (p, x), (q, y), (r, z) ∈ I × F. In the following we write also τB×f F= τ. The time seperation function has the following properties:\n\n- τ (y, y ′\n) = 0 if y ′\nand y are not cuassally related,\n\n- τ (y, y ′\n) > 0 if y ≪ y\n′. The generalized Lorentzian cone w.r.t. −I, the metric space F and the function f : I → (0, ∞) is\n\n(I × F, D, ≪, ≤, τ) =: −I ×fF.\n\nIf F is an intrinsic metric space, then −I ×fF is a Lorentzian pre-length space in the sense of [KS18]. Here we collect some properties of −I ×fF. We always assume that F is intrinsic.\n\n- −I ×fF has the push-up property: if (s, x) ≪ (t, y) if and only if there exists a future directed causal curve from (s, x) to (t, y) with positive length. Moreover I\n+/− ((s, x)) is open for all (s, t) ∈ I × F.\n\n- If F is geodesic, then J\n± ((s, x)) is closed for all (s, x) ∈ I × F. The next theorem is the Lorentzian analogue of Theorem 2.5 above.\n\nTheorem 3.1 (Kunzinger, Saemann, Graf, Alexander). Let X be a strictly intrinsic metric space and let γ = (α, β) be a future directed, causal curve that is a maximizer w.r.t. L in −I ×fF and parametrized proportional to arclength. Then\n\n(a) β is a minimizer in F;\n(b) (Fiber independence) α is independent of F, except for the total height, i.e. the length L\nF\n\n(β) of β. More precisely, if Fˆ is another\nstrictly intrinsic metric space and βˆ is a minimizing geodesic in F¯ with the same length and speed as β, then (α, βˆ) is a minimizer in −I ×fFˆ. c\n\n|||for a constant c|.|\n|---|---|---|---|\n||2f ◦α||γ|\n|1 ′ 2|1◦α|||\n|2|2f|||\n|||f||\n|f||||\n||||f|\n\n(c) If γ is timelike, then β has speed\n2f2γ◦α γ\n\n(d) If γ is timelike, then − (α) +2= E for a constant E. We stated above that a generalized cone −I × F over an intrinsic metric\nspace F is a Lorentzian pre-length space. If F is locally compact, it was shown in [AGKS23] that −I × F is even a Lorentzian length spaces.\n\nTheorem 3.2. Let F be a locally compact length space. Then −I × F is a strongly causal Lorentzian length space.\n",
